From cb19a1476a2bd42c4daff715ccde56ef975458a2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 11:40:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08049/11362] Best-of-N: criterion (iii) is 0 at N=8 because
 (i) and (ii) never co-occur, not because no candidate plans

Phase 1 as asked: N candidates per STALL induction, selected by the verifier, scored OUT-OF-SAMPLE
against the proven rendered split. Five stall games (25 collected transitions, 0 levels banked) x 8
candidates = 40; vc33 is partitioned out as the post-bank contrast (2 levels, 1 transition, 0
held-out rows -- the vacuity trap). One llama-server process for all 48 candidates, CUDA build
proven from /proc/<pid>/exe, n_ctx 32768, temperature fixed at the shipped 0.2 so N is a pure
sampling axis.

THE ANSWER, AND IT IS NOT THE NULL THE PRE-REGISTERED READING WOULD HAVE PRODUCED. Criterion (iii)
as posed -- a conjunction onto (i) -- is 0.0 at N=1, N=4 and N=8, and 0 of 40 marginally. The
pre-registered reading of that zero was "selection is not the fixable part." That reading is wrong
here and shipping it would have been a false null. The zero is an EMPTY INTERSECTION: 9 of 40
candidates clear (i), 2 of 40 reach a satisfiable goal with a found plan, and NO candidate is in
both sets. Drop the conjunction and best-of-N works -- unconditional (iii) goes 0.0 at N=1 to 0.4
at N=4 and stays there; the SHIPPED pipeline's own conjunction (trust gate AND goal AND plan) goes
0.0 to 0.2. The plannable candidates are ft09 k1 (plan length 2, and it PASSES the shipped trust
gate) and tn36 k1 (plan length 6).

Selecting on dynamics is anti-selective for plannability, which is the design tension resolved
against the cheap criterion. On tn36 the SIX candidates with held-out accuracy 1.0 -- 17 of 17
held-out changing transitions predicted exactly -- ALL fail the goal gate; the single candidate
whose goal is satisfiable and plannable scores 0.235 (4 of 17). The mechanism is visible in the
predicates: the accuracy-1.0 engines induce `np.all(grid[1, 1:62] == 3)`, 61 cells needing far more
than the shipped max_depth=40, while k1 induces `grid[1, 56:62] == 3`, six cells, reachable. The
pooled means (0.518 satisfiable vs 0.674 not) are n=2 against n=29 and carry no weight; the empty
intersection and the per-game tn36 contrast do, and neither depends on pooling.

Sampling adds candidates but not GAMES, and the gap says why. Criterion (i)'s per-game yield is
flat at 0.4 for every N, while its marginal per-candidate rate is 0.225 -- under independence
any-of-8 would be 0.87. Observed 0.40 against implied 0.87 means (i)-success is a property of the
GAME, not of the draw: tn36 and lp85 produce clean engines repeatedly, ft09/sc25/tu93 never do.

What the zeros are made of, kept separate because they license opposite conclusions. Of the 9
candidates clearing (i), 6 failed the goal gate UNDECIDED (`goal_unreached_within_depth` -- nodes
discarded unexpanded at the cap, so reachability is UNKNOWN) and only 3 were DISPROVED
(`degenerate_goal_predicate`, frontier exhausted). No threshold was widened to manufacture a pass,
and `plan_in_model` was run WITHOUT goal-energy guidance, so (iii) is a lower bound on what the
live best-first planner could find.

A PRODUCTION HANG, found by being bitten by it. The generation loop wedged for 13 minutes in state
R at 32% CPU with no open socket and both GPUs idle, spinning inside `validate_engine_code`'s dry
run of a non-terminating induced engine (ft09 candidate 5, seed 7105, which had rambled to the full
4096-token cap). This is NOT harness-only: the shipped `generate()` calls the same check in-process
via `_engine_defects` -> `validate_engine_code` -> `dry_run_defects`, which executes LLM-written
code with no wall-clock bound, so a non-terminating induced engine hangs a live induction. A signal
alarm cannot fix it -- `_engine_defects` swallows exceptions to [] and `dry_run_defects` catches
broadly, so an in-process timeout would be absorbed and become a false CLEAN. Every execution of
generated code in this harness now happens in a killable subprocess; the candidate is recorded as
`validation_timeout` and not usable.

Cost, since the ask was per-GPU-hour and not per-attempt: 45.9 s mean per candidate, 52.2 / 170.9 /
367.1 GPU-seconds per induction at N=1/4/8. The unconditional (iii) gain from N=1 to N=4 costs 3.3x
the decode. Gate + planner are CPU and are reported separately.

Generated .py intermediates are written outside the repo. An in-tree copy put model completions in
front of this repo's ruff pre-commit hook, and an auto-formatted completion is no longer the thing
that was measured; the record is the completion .txt plus its recorded sha.

Reproducibility: gate results are cached by code sha, so adding a criterion re-analyses 48
candidates without re-executing a single search. adversarial_verify: 0 flagged.

